  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $y=-9ln\u2061x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln\u2061x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9ln\u2061x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln\u2061x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
